// Generated from packages/ui/src/docs/*.md by `npm run docs:build`. Do not edit by hand.

/**
 * The design system's prose, as data both platforms can read.
 *
 * The web renders these through Storybook's own `<Markdown>` block, so its docs look exactly
 * as they did when the same words lived in `.mdx`. The native playbook renders them with its
 * own small renderer. One source, two renderers — which is the only arrangement in which the
 * two surfaces cannot start saying different things.
 */
export const DOC_PAGES = {
  CHAT_GUIDELINES: "# Chat\n\nA chat surface, in two halves. This section is ONE CATEGORY HOLDING TWO FAMILIES, and the split is\nthe shape of the surface rather than a filing choice: `composer` is everything a user writes a\nmessage with, `message` is everything above it — the transcript, the rows in it, and the scroll\nmachinery that makes a live reply follow the reader. Two disjoint sets of components, so two guard\ntriples reconcile against them rather than one guard trying to cover both.\n\nOpen it when you are building somewhere a conversation happens.\n\n`ChatComposer` owns the input frame and arrangement, and `ChatThread` owns the transcript frame, so\nadopting those two whole is the default path. The parts are exported because they have genuinely\ndifferent lifetimes and consumers — a surface with an unusual toolbar builds its own arrangement\nfrom the same buttons rather than growing the shell a prop per variation, and a message row is\nuseful on a card or a docs page with no transcript around it.\n\n## Use this, not that\n\n**`ChatToolsButton` is the `+`, `ChatAttachmentButton` is the paperclip.** Figma's `02 Tools`\nset publishes three types — Mic, Tools, Send — and its word \"Tools\" names the paperclip. The\napp has four controls, and the `+` that opens image, video and optimize is not the paperclip\nthat opens the file picker. The published name is what changed; the treatment and both glyphs\nare as drawn.\n\n**On `ChatSendButton`, use the `state` axis — there is no `active` boolean.** Send is an\naction, not a switch, so it emits no `aria-pressed`. This shipped once with `active`\ndocumented as the in-flight look while drawing the ordinary send glyph, which left a composer\non a surface that streams every reply with no way to become a stop button. `stop` and\n`waiting` are what replaced it.\n\n**Do not paint a `ChatDrawer` that sits inside a composer.** Its `variant` defaults to\n`inherit` and paints nothing, because the tray behind it already carries the tint from\n`ChatComposer.tone`. The opaque values are for a drawer used outside a composer, which has no\ntray to inherit from.\n\n**Render an attachment preview with a plain element, not the library `Image`.** That component\nrefuses any source which is not remote https, and a composer's previews are local blobs and\nbase64 by definition. The same carve-out applies to a GENERATED image in a message: those arrive\nas data URLs, object-store keys and presigned links. `Image` plus `Lightbox` are the right pair\nonce a picture has a settled https home.\n\n**Wrap a non-bubble child in a GHOST BUBBLE when it has to read as part of the conversation.**\nThis is the placement contract for artifacts, and it is a padding rule rather than a component. A\ngenerated image, a video, an attachment chip or an artifact card renders as ordinary children of\n`ChatMessage`; dropped in raw it sits flush with the message edge, a whole inset to the side of the\nsentence above it — two leading edges in one message, which reads as a layout accident. A ghost\nbubble drops the fill and the vertical padding and KEEPS the side padding, so the child's leading\nedge meets the text column. Add `width=\"full\"` when it should span the message column rather than\nstop at the bubble's own cap. Leaving a child raw is the right answer exactly when it is MEANT to\nbleed — a full-width card closing a turn — and `ChatMessageBubble`'s `GhostAlignment` story shows\nboth side by side.\n\n**Put a timestamp on the LAST BUBBLE's metadata slot, not the message's, when a turn ends with an\nartifact.** Both slots exist for this: a message-level timestamp sits under everything in the row,\nincluding a card, and reads as belonging to the card rather than to the turn.\n\n**Let `ChatThread` own the scroller; `ChatMessageList` must not.** The list is presentational —\nit announces, spaces and aligns, and publishes its inner element to the frame. Two scrollers in one\ntranscript means the follow scrolls one box while the reader is looking at another, and every class\nname on both stays correct.\n\n## Composer members\n\n- **ChatComposer** — the shell: the tray, the frame and the arrangement, with a `tone` axis\n  and a `sendDisabled` boolean the caller owns.\n- **ChatComposerInput** — the text editor. A real `<textarea>` that autogrows, never a\n  `contentEditable`.\n- **ChatDrawer** — the region above the editor. A container for arbitrary children, which\n  renders nothing at all when empty.\n- **ChatAttachment** — one attachment chip, `image` or `file`, with an optional size and a\n  status for an upload in flight.\n- **ChatToolsButton** — the `+` menu trigger. The one button with no published source.\n- **ChatAttachmentButton** — the paperclip.\n- **ChatVoiceButton** — the microphone, and the only button that goes red.\n- **ChatSendButton** — send, stop or waiting.\n\n## Message members\n\n- **ChatThread** — the transcript frame. It owns the scroller, follows a streaming reply,\n  publishes its scroll container to the list inside, and pins the return-to-bottom affordance at\n  its foot.\n- **ChatMessageList** — the log region. It announces politely, marks itself busy only while a\n  reply streams, flows a `density` to everything inside, seats a short conversation above the\n  composer, and loads older messages when the reader reaches the top.\n- **ChatMessage** — one row: a `sender`, a `density`, and slots for an avatar, a name and\n  metadata. It takes NO message object.\n- **ChatMessageBubble** — the visible bubble, with grouping, a width override and the ghost\n  presentation the placement rule above depends on. It returns a FRAGMENT, not a wrapper.\n- **ChatMessageMetadata** — a timestamp, arbitrary footer content and a delivery status in one\n  row. Renders nothing at all when given nothing.\n- **ChatScrollButton** — the return-to-bottom pill, circular without a visible label and\n  expanded with one.\n\nThe whole set assembled into the product's own chat room — shell, transcript, generated images, a\nvideo, the action row and the composer — is the **Examples / TemanAI chat** page. Read it before\nadopting any of these: it is the only page that shows the pieces beside each other, and its\ndocblock lists what the app still owns (the page background, the reading measure, the error and\ncredit overlays, and two icons this registry does not publish).\n\n`useChatStreamScroll` is the family's one public hook and the whole of the scroll seam. It has no\ncontract entry, because the contract is component-keyed and an entry would demand a story file for\na thing with nothing to show.\n\n## Worth knowing\n\n`ChatVoiceButton`'s active fill is red where the other three are blue, and the contract records\nthat it must not be collapsed onto one accent constant: red says the microphone is live, blue\nsays the next turn is armed, and a \"consolidation\" onto one colour compiles, renders, and turns\na recording indicator into an arming one. Its guard asserts the disagreement.\n\nThe composer's send label and handler are the host's, and in `stop` that is load-bearing rather\nthan stylistic: a button drawing a stop square while still announcing \"Kirim pesan\" and still\ncalling send is worse than one that never changed.\n\n**The message family takes no message object anywhere, and that is the only design that works\nhere.** Three surfaces in this product carry three incompatible message shapes, so a component\naccepting any one of them would be adoptable on exactly one surface. These own chrome and slots;\nthe content is children. Marker parsing stays in the app for the same reason — it is the wire\nprotocol, and a design system should not know that a marker exists.\n\n**The scroll lock is positional, not event-sourced.** It releases when the reader's position moves\naway from the bottom, not when a wheel or touch event fires: an event listener misses a keyboard\nscroll and a scrollbar drag entirely and fires on momentum frames nobody intended. Four hand-built\nscroll implementations exist across this product and no two agree on their thresholds, which is\nthe drift this seam exists to end — and reconciling any particular surface's behaviour with it is\nan adoption decision with a reader-visible outcome, not a library one.\n\nBoth families ship UNCONSUMED — nothing in `apps/` imports either yet — and adoption will be a\nvisual change rather than like-for-like. The composer's geometry is Figma's and the app's current\nchrome has no responsive sizing at all; the message family has no published design source to\ndiverge from, so every one of its axes is greenfield and each entry records that rather than\nleaving the absence to read as an unchecked box.\n",
  CONTENT_GUIDELINES: "# Content\n\nThe pieces a page is built out of — the containers, the rules, the labels on things and the\nmedia inside them. Open this section when you are asking *what do I put this in*, or *what do\nI mark it with*, rather than how a user gives you a value.\n\n## Use this, not that\n\n**A `Chip` when it is pressable, a `Badge` when it is not.** They look alike and the\ndifference is structural: `Badge` renders a `<span>`, takes no handler and has no focus ring,\nand its docblock says that is the point rather than an omission — five of its six call sites\nhad passed an `onClick`, which is what made them chips. `Chip` renders a real `<button>`, so\nit is reachable by keyboard and announced as a control.\n\n**A `Card` for a container, not for a course.** The published Figma set named Card is a course\ncard — it publishes a rating row, a struck price, a best-seller flag and tags. The contract\nrecords that it was read and deliberately not adopted; ours takes a variant and a padding\nladder and nothing else. The course card is a composition on top, and already exists in the\napp as `CourseCard.tsx`.\n\n**`Image` only for a remote https source.** It refuses anything else, as an SSRF control. Both\n`Lightbox` and `ChatAttachment` record in the contract that they render a plain element instead\nprecisely because that refusal would blank every local blob and base64 preview.\n\n## Members\n\n- **Badge** — a non-interactive status or category mark. `bestseller` is a solid success\n  surface where the other colours are tints, which is why its text inverts.\n- **Chip** — the pressable sibling, with a `default`/`active` state and a size ramp that opens\n  at `huge`.\n- **Card** — the container: an `elevated`, `outlined` or `plain` treatment and a padding step.\n- **Divider** — a rule, solid or dashed, horizontal or vertical. The vertical one is a\n  different element rather than a rotated one, since it takes its height from the row.\n- **List** — the list renderer. Its direction is a layout choice, deliberately not contracted\n  as an axis.\n- **Disclosure** — a show/hide section. No Figma set exists; this is the design-it-first case.\n- **Image** — the media element, with a `natural` type beyond Figma's fixed thumbnail and\n  banner ratios, and `fit` kept as a separate prop from `type`.\n- **CodeBlock** — a fenced code block with a language header and a copy control. Named for its\n  content rather than its affordance, which leaves room for syntax highlighting later.\n\n## Worth knowing\n\n`Card`'s `variant` axis was dropped once because no consumer passed it, then restored when a\nsurvey of the TemanAI surfaces found all three treatments live. The contract keeps the reason\non the entry: \"no consumer\" is evidence about adoption, never about need.\n",
  FEEDBACK_GUIDELINES: "# Feedback\n\nTelling the user what happened, or that something is still happening. Open this section when\nyou are asking *how do I say this went wrong*, *how do I show this is loading*, or *where does\nthis bit of explanation go*.\n\n## Use this, not that\n\n**`Alert` is the element; `Toaster` is the host.** They are one component and one surface, not\ntwo ways of saying the same thing. A raised toast renders `Alert` at `type='toast'`, so the\nseverity and size vocabulary lives on the `Alert` entry and `Toaster` does not restate it. The\ncontract also records why dismissal belongs to the host: an Alert rendered inline has nothing\nto dismiss it from, and the toast host already owns the queue, the timer and the close control,\nso a second one on the element would give two things the power to remove one toast.\n\n**A `Skeleton` when you know the shape, a `Spinner` when you do not.** `Skeleton` is\ndeliberately `aria-hidden` and announces nothing — the surface owning the loading state does\nthe announcing — while `Spinner` carries `role=\"status\"` and a spoken `label`, because the\nmovement is not a channel every reader has and under reduced motion there is no movement at\nall. Passing an empty label is a real choice there, distinct from omitting one: a spinner\ninside a button would otherwise concatenate its text into that button's accessible name.\n\n**A `ProgressBar` when there is a value, not a `Spinner`.** `ProgressBar` shares its value,\nmaximum, clamping and announced semantics across both its shapes; only the drawing differs\nbetween the bar and the gauge.\n\n## Members\n\n- **Alert** — the severity box, inline or as a toast, with a `treatment` axis controlling how\n  much of the box the severity paints.\n- **Toaster** — the toast host. `position` is its only real axis; the queue cap and the\n  auto-dismiss delay are numbers.\n- **Tooltip** — a tip on a trigger, dark or white. It has no arrow prop: the arrow follows the\n  resolved placement after collision flipping.\n- **Skeleton** — one shape primitive and one shimmer, in `text`, `circle` or `rect`.\n- **Spinner** — the indeterminate loading indicator, with a spoken label.\n- **ProgressBar** — determinate progress, `linear` or `radial`.\n\n## Worth knowing\n\n**The success toast is the filled check; the inline success Alert is the outline.** Figma draws\nthe toast's tick knocked out of a white disc so the green reads through it, and the inline box\nwith lucide's outline on a tint — two drawings for two jobs, and neither name may be repointed at\nthe other. The fork lives in `Alert` itself, on both platforms, so `ALERT_VARIANT_ICON` still\nanswers only for the inline box; a \"consolidation\" that moves `success` in that table moves the\ninline Alert too, and nothing but the pinning test would notice.\n\n**A raised toast is Medium at every width on the web** (14/20 text, 24px glyph, 12px side padding,\ncopy at weight 500 as Figma draws it); the close glyph beside it is 20px. Pass `size` to pick\nLarge or Small. Native still follows `useWindowDimensions` — Medium from 768 up, Small below — so\nthe two platforms now differ on a phone, by decision (UMK-957). The inline `Alert` keeps its own default.\n\n`Tooltip`'s `showClose` defaults off even though Figma defaults it on: a tip that closes on\npointer-leave has nothing for a close button to do. And its `children` is typed\n`ReactElement` rather than `ReactNode` on purpose — Radix's `asChild` calls `Children.only`,\nso the narrower type is the only compile-time signal there is.\n\n`Spinner` and `ProgressBar` disagree on how their size steps are spelled — abbreviated on one,\nspelled out on the other. That is recorded rather than repaired: changing a shipped vocabulary\nbelongs to the slice that owns reconciling both.\n",
  FORM_GUIDELINES: "# Form\n\nThe controls a user types into or chooses from. Open this section when you are asking *how\ndoes this value get entered* — a string, a number, a date, a file, one choice out of several,\nor an on/off setting. Every control here owns its own label: the contract records that\ndecision on `TextField` and applies it to the whole family, because a shared field wrapper is\na second component a caller can forget, and forgetting it yields an unlabelled control that\nstill renders perfectly.\n\n## Use this, not that\n\n**A `Switch`, not a `CheckBox`, when the change applies immediately.** The contract states\nthis as the reason `Switch` carries neither an error boolean nor a `required` prop: a toggle\napplies its change on the spot, so there is nothing to validate against. A setting that has\nto be saved before it takes effect is a checkbox.\n\n**A `Select`, not a `Menu`, when there is a value to hold.** `Menu` carries items with their\nown handlers and an optional per-row tick, but no `value`/`onValueChange` pair — the contract\nsays so directly — so a caller using it for selection holds the selection itself and the menu\nnever reflects it.\n\n**`Label` only where no control owns one.** Because every slice-2 control renders its own\nlabel from a `label` prop, the standalone `Label` is for a group heading or something that is\nnot a control at all.\n\n## Members\n\n- **TextField** — the single-line and multiline text input.\n- **Select** — a value picker with an optional type-to-search; the list renders in place\n  rather than portalled.\n- **CheckBox** — a box that can also be `indeterminate`, kept as a boolean because a\n  part-selected parent is neither checked nor unchecked.\n- **Radio** — one choice out of a group; no indeterminate boolean, because there is no partial\n  selection to express.\n- **Switch** — an immediately-applied on/off, with a `loading` boolean for a change in flight.\n- **Slider** — a single-value control with min, max and step. Figma publishes the track and\n  thumb geometry (`Slider` 1308:8026) and nothing else, so the states are ours.\n- **DateTimeInput** — date and time entry, web only; the contract records that the platform\n  picker is the right answer away from the browser.\n- **Upload** — a dropzone with a status ramp, a preview slot and a drag-over treatment that\n  composes over the resolved status.\n- **SelectionCard** — the agent surface's choice picker, shaped against the option payloads\n  that surface renders. Its selection value is an array even in single-select. `layout=\"tile\"`\n  is a pack picker's card: content centred and the control drawn in the top-right corner, over\n  the same hidden real input.\n- **Label** — the standalone form label, with `required` carrying the mandatory mark.\n\n## Worth knowing\n\n`Slider` and `DateTimeInput` are the two controls whose label prop is a union of a visible\nlabel **or** an accessible name. The compile-time gate stays — omitting both still fails to\nbuild — but the visible half is given up on the name arm, because the A2UI wire format feeding\nthose two carries no label at all. The other controls still require the visible label.\n\n`Upload` records four states as divergences that Figma publishes none of: drag-over, busy,\npreview and the dashed border. Each is written as its own entry so a reader can tell \"we added\nthis\" from \"Figma has this\", per state.\n",
  GETTING_STARTED: "# The UMKMall design system\n\nThis is `@umkmall/ui` — the component library the web app, the admin dashboard and the React\nNative app all render from. This Storybook is its reference: every component here is the real\none, imported from source, not a mock of it.\n\n## Read this first\n\nThe library is built around **one contract**. `packages/ui/src/contract/variants.ts` declares\nwhat axes every component has — which variants, which sizes, which states — and nothing else\nin the repo is allowed to disagree with it. A component maps a contract entry to its own\nclasses; the contract itself holds no Tailwind and imports nothing, which is what lets the\nnative renderer consume the same file unchanged.\n\nThat has a practical consequence worth knowing before you write any code against this\nlibrary: **if a value is not in the contract, the component does not accept it.** A size that\nlooks like it should exist and does not is a deliberate absence, not an oversight — check the\ncomponent's entry before reaching for a class override at the call site.\n\n## Install and run\n\nThe library is a workspace package; nothing to install separately.\n\n```bash\nnpm install                       # once, from the repo root\nnpm run dev -w @umkmall/design    # this Storybook, on :3003\n```\n\nTwo commands matter while you work in it:\n\n```bash\nnpm run test -w @umkmall/ui       # the guards — see Contributing\nnpm run typecheck -w @umkmall/design\n```\n\nThe second one is not optional and not redundant. `packages/ui`'s own tsconfig **excludes**\n`*.stories.tsx`, and `storybook build` transpiles without typechecking — so a story with a\ntype error passes both the library's typecheck and the build, and only this command sees it.\n\n## Import from the barrel\n\n```tsx\nimport { Button, Text, Alert } from '@umkmall/ui';\n```\n\nReact Native imports from a sibling entry point, which publishes the same names against the\nsame contract:\n\n```tsx\nimport { Button, Text, Alert } from '@umkmall/ui/native';\n```\n\nThe two are deliberately not identical. `Tooltip` and `DateTimeInput` have no native sibling\nand are absent by design; `Menu` is a bottom sheet on native and a dropdown on web. Absence\nis an answer there, the same way it is in the contract.\n\n## How this Storybook is organised\n\n**Foundations** holds the design tokens — colour, the type scale, layout and spacing — plus\nthe three components that are closer to tokens than to widgets: `Text`, `Icon` and `Button`.\nStart here if you are looking for a value rather than a component.\n\n**Components** holds everything else, in seven sections. Each one opens with a Guidelines\npage saying what belongs in it and which component to reach for:\n\n- **Form** — the controls a user types into or chooses from.\n- **Content** — the pieces a page is built out of.\n- **Navigation** — moving between views, and showing where you are.\n- **Feedback** — telling the user what happened.\n- **Overlays** — anything that renders above the page.\n- **Shell** — the chrome around a screen.\n- **Chat** — the composer and its parts, which are one surface rather than general widgets.\n\nThe section a component sits in answers *where do I look for this*, and is decided on that\nbasis alone — it is not the same thing as which guard pair covers it in the test suite. Two\ncomponents sit somewhere their test family would not have put them, and that disagreement is\ndeliberate.\n\n**Guidelines**, **Patterns** and **Contributing** are the prose sections: how to choose\nbetween components, how to compose them, and what changing one costs.\n\n## Every page has a props table\n\nEach component's **Docs** page is generated from its TypeScript, so the props table is the\nreal prop surface — including the parts that are hard to read in source. `Button`'s icon-only\nform is a discriminated union that will not compile without an accessible name; `Image`\naccepts `alt` or `decorative`, never both; the form family shares one labelling union where\npassing both a visible label and an `aria-label` is a type error. Those show up as unions in\nthe table rather than as `any`, which is the whole reason the docs are wired to TypeScript.\n\n**Show code** under any example prints the JSX. On a state-matrix story it prints a single\ncanonical usage instead of the grid scaffolding — the grid is how the page proves coverage,\nnot how you would write it.\n",
  GUIDELINES: "# Guidelines\n\nThe rules that hold across the whole library. Each of the seven **Components** sections opens\nwith its own Guidelines page covering the choices specific to it; this page covers the ones\nthat are not specific to anything.\n\n## The contract is the vocabulary\n\n`packages/ui/src/contract/variants.ts` declares what axes every component has. Before this\nfile, every primitive declared its own variant block inline and nothing compared them — which\nis how two components end up with size ramps that disagree about what `medium` means.\n\nThree things follow from that, and they are the ones most often got wrong:\n\n**An axis value that is not in the contract does not exist.** If a component has three sizes\nand you need a fourth, the change is a contract entry and a component that reads it — not a\nclass override at the call site. An override at the call site is invisible to every guard in\nthis repo and to everyone reading the component later.\n\n**The contract holds vocabulary, never Tailwind.** No classes, no `cva`, no React, no DOM\ntypes, not even an import. `variants.platform-neutral.test.ts` walks the import graph and\nfails on any bare specifier, so this is enforced rather than remembered. It is also why the\nReact Native renderer consumes the same file unchanged.\n\n**A departure from Figma is recorded, not hidden.** Where this repo deliberately differs from\nwhat the design file publishes, the entry carries a `divergences` record saying what Figma\ncalls it, what we do instead, and why. An undocumented divergence reads as a mistake and gets\n\"corrected\" back into Figma's shape by the next person through — which is exactly what the\nrecord exists to prevent. If you are about to make a component match Figma more closely, read\nits divergences first; you may be undoing a decision.\n\n## Absence is an answer\n\nSeveral things in this library are missing on purpose, and each absence has a reason written\ndown beside it:\n\n- `display` and `h1` publish Bold and Semibold only. Offering `medium` on them would resolve\n  to no token and render a browser default, which looks like a styling bug rather than a\n  missing style.\n- `Tooltip` and `DateTimeInput` have no React Native sibling.\n- Eight contract entries carry no `family`, meaning no family guard pair covers them.\n  `unfamilied-coverage.test.ts` names each one together with the guard **files** that are the\n  whole of its coverage — including one row that says plainly there is no dedicated guard at\n  all. A recorded gap is not the same as a claim of coverage.\n\nTreat a gap as deliberate until you have read the note saying otherwise.\n\n## Accessibility is in the type, where it can be\n\nWhere an accessibility requirement can be made unbuildable, this library makes it\nunbuildable rather than documenting it:\n\n- `Button`'s icon-only form is a union that does not compile without an accessible name. A\n  nameless icon button is not a lint warning here; it is a type error.\n- `Image` accepts `alt` **or** `decorative`, never both and never neither.\n- The form family shares one labelling union: a visible `label` or an `aria-label`, not both.\n  Passing both is how a field ends up announcing one thing and showing another.\n\nTwo of those unions have a runtime companion, because a plain-JavaScript caller can still do\nthe thing the type forbids. Where you see one, do not \"simplify\" it away on the grounds that\nthe type already covers it.\n\n## Language\n\nInterface copy is Indonesian. Story labels, example strings and placeholder content in this\nStorybook follow the app, not the code. Prop names, token names and everything in the\ncontract stay English.\n\n## Colour comes from tokens, and a class that resolves can still be wrong\n\nEvery colour in the library resolves through the token pipeline. The utilities are split per\nutility, which means `Text` and `Surface` publish many of the same sub-paths and disagree on\nseveral of them — so `text-default-primary` and `bg-default-primary` are a well-formed pair\nholding two completely different colours.\n\nEvery gate in this repo proves a class **resolves**. None of them proves it resolves to the\nright hex. A mistyped palette is green everywhere and only visible in a browser, which is why\na colour change is one of the few things here that has to be looked at rather than tested.\n",
  NAVIGATION_GUIDELINES: "# Navigation\n\nMoving between views, and showing where you are. Two components, and they answer different\nquestions: `Tabs` lets a reader choose which of several peer views to see, while `Stepper`\nshows progress through an ordered sequence the reader is being taken along.\n\n## Use this, not that\n\n**Do not reach for a per-item \"active\" prop in either.** In `Tabs`, which tab is active is a\nproperty of the set: the contract drops Figma's `State` axis from the trigger and reads it off\nthe shared context instead, because a per-trigger prop makes two simultaneously-active tabs —\nand a set with none — representable, and both render perfectly. `Stepper` is the other way\nround for a stated reason: a step's `state` and its `position` are separate axes, because\nFigma's mark set folds a position value into a state enum, which makes \"done and mid\"\nunsayable while \"mid and default\" is sayable twice.\n\nThere is no second real \"use this, not that\" here. With two members that do unrelated jobs,\ninventing a third would be the fabrication the contract exists to prevent.\n\n## Members\n\n- **Tabs** — a tab set with a size ramp, an optional leading icon, an optional title and a\n  `notif` count. Supplying the number is what shows the badge; the boolean Figma publishes\n  could not say what the badge displays.\n- **Stepper** — a step trail, horizontal or vertical, with `default`, `current` and `done`\n  states. `position` is derivable from the index and kept explicit anyway, so a single step\n  rendered in isolation can say it has no leg behind it.\n\n## Worth knowing\n\n`Stepper` has two consumers: the journey run page's stage trail and the account-deletion flow. A\nstep that passes `onSelect` draws its mark and label as one button (UMK-1809) with the connector\noutside it; one without it renders as before, with no button. Its vocabulary was first validated\nagainst the design source alone, and the recorded divergences keep a revision cheap.\n\n`Tabs` was keyed `Tab` in the contract until UMK-979, because the axes Figma publishes describe\none tab rather than the set. The axis values still describe one tab; the entry name now matches\nthe component, its story and its native sibling.\n",
  OVERLAYS_GUIDELINES: "# Overlays\n\nAnything that renders above the page. Open this section when you are asking *where does this\ngo if it does not belong in the flow* — a modal decision, a list of actions, or an image opened\nfull-bleed.\n\n## Use this, not that\n\n**A `Dialog` for a modal surface, and a `Lightbox` for an image.** The contract is explicit\nthat `Lightbox` composes the Radix dialog directly rather than widening `Dialog`: `Dialog`'s\npresentation is fixed by decision and its modality claim is hardcoded because it has no\nnon-modal form, so bending it to serve a full-bleed viewer would reverse both. The viewer also\nrenders its image with a plain element rather than through the library `Image`, whose\nrefusal to accept a non-remote-https source is correct there and would blank every base64\npreview here.\n\n**A `Menu` for actions, not for a value.** Its rows carry their own handlers and an optional\ntick, but there is no `value`/`onValueChange` pair — the `Select` entry records that directly\nas the reason Menu cannot substitute for it.\n\n## Members\n\n- **Dialog** — the modal: four width steps, a bottom sheet below the medium breakpoint and a\n  centred card from there up, with `centered` forcing the card at every width.\n- **Menu** — a popover list of rows, in a `pill` or a `boxed` treatment. Rows may nest a\n  submenu or carry a destination, rendered through an app-supplied link component.\n- **Lightbox** — the full-bleed image viewer. Its two affordances are callbacks rather than\n  variants: supply one and its control appears.\n\n## Worth knowing\n\n`Menu` has no published Figma set at all, so its two treatments were read off two shipped\nsurfaces — `pill` from the chat composer's tools menu, `boxed` from the user-settings popover.\nNeither is wrong with nothing published, which is why the settings popover was not restyled\nonto the composer's look; the axis is where a designer reconciling the two later lands. The\nStorybook render is what a designer works against.\n\n`Dialog` had no published set either, so its four-step width ladder was read off the code it\nreplaces. `Lightbox` deliberately has no zoom, no pan, no gallery and no drag gesture: the\ncomponent it replaces has none, and drag-to-dismiss stays module-private inside `Dialog`.\n\nBoth `Menu` and `Lightbox` state their emptiness explicitly rather than incidentally. Inventing\na multi-value axis to satisfy the structural guard is exactly the fabrication the contract\nexists to prevent.\n",
  PATTERNS: "# Patterns\n\nHow these components go together. Every pattern here is one the library already had to get\nright for a real screen — not a suggestion.\n\n## The app frame\n\n`AppShell` is four slots and the arrangement between them: a full-height column that does not\nitself scroll, a header that does not shrink, a navigation column beside the content, and a\ncontent region whose overflow chain is pinned so **your** scroller is the thing that scrolls.\n\n```tsx\n<AppShell\n  header={({ scrolled }) => <Header title=\"Kelas saya\" scrolled={scrolled} showBack onBack={goBack} />}\n  sidebar={<Sidebar label=\"Navigasi utama\" />}\n  routeKey={pathname}\n>\n  <main>{children}</main>\n</AppShell>\n```\n\nWhat it deliberately does **not** reach for: a store, a router, a feature gate, an onboarding\ncomponent. Every one of those is a real thing an app has above its shell, and every one of\nthem is slot content rather than a dependency — a library frame that imported a router would\nbe unusable by the second app that wanted a frame. That import graph is asserted, not merely\nintended.\n\nThe `header` slot takes a function as well as a node, and that is how `Header.scrolled` gets\nfed without the shell knowing what a header is. `routeKey` clears the scroll flag on\nnavigation; omit it in a single-surface shell.\n\nThe one piece of behaviour the shell owns is the scroll spy that lets a header change\ntreatment once the content has moved. It has to be here: scroll events do not bubble, the\nscroller belongs to whatever you put in `children`, and the only way to observe it from the\nframe is a capturing listener. Two surfaces in the app arrived at that independently, which\nis why it moved into the library rather than being predicted into it.\n\n## A form field\n\nReach for the control's own `label`, not a separate `Label` beside it.\n\n```tsx\n<TextField label=\"Nama usaha\" value={name} onChange={(e) => setName(e.target.value)} />\n```\n\nEvery control in the Form section owns its label — `TextField`'s is not even optional, because\na label prop cannot be forgotten the way a sibling `<label>` can. `Label` is the standalone form, for a group\nheading, a fieldset, or a non-control that still needs a name — putting one above a\n`TextField` gives that field two names.\n\nThe labelling type is a union: a visible `label`, **or** an `aria-label`, never both. That is\nwhat stops a field announcing one thing and showing another. Four controls do not accept the\n`aria-label` arm at all and still require the visible label; that restriction is asserted in\nthe type tests, so the compiler will tell you which.\n\n## Telling the user what happened\n\nTwo shapes, one component. `Alert` renders in place; a toast is the same visual raised into\na host.\n\n```tsx\n<Toaster />                          // once, near the root of the app\n\ntoast('Perubahan tersimpan', { variant: 'success' });\n```\n\nOn the web, mount `Toaster` exactly once. It owns a single live region, and the toast path\ndeliberately renders `Alert` with its own `role` suppressed — nesting an assertive `role=\"alert\"`\ninside the host's polite live region produces either a double announcement or a swallowed one,\ninconsistently across screen readers. A test asserting \"a role is present\" passes either way,\nwhich is why the guard counts regions instead.\n\nOn React Native a sheet is its own native window (`Modal`), so the root host draws *beneath* an\nopen sheet. A sheet that must show a toast over itself mounts a second `Toaster` inside it. Only\nthe most recently mounted host draws — the others render nothing and queue no exits — so there is\nstill one toast and one live region at a time, and the root host takes the queue back when the\nsheet closes.\n\nUse an inline `Alert` when the message belongs to a place on the page and should stay there;\nuse `toast` when it belongs to an action the user just took.\n\n## Choosing a treatment for an Alert\n\n`Alert` carries a `treatment` axis that Figma does not publish — `full`, `accent`, `coach`,\n`banner`. It exists because the app's own callout had six looks the component could not\nexpress, and most live instances are the left-accent shape rather than the filled one. Its\ncolours are built from the published callout token group, so they are read rather than\ninvented.\n\n`full` is pinned byte-identical to what shipped before the axis existed. If you are matching\nan older screen, that is the one.\n\n## Overlays\n\nEverything in the Overlays section renders above the page, and each one is a different\nquestion:\n\n- `Dialog` interrupts: the user must deal with it before continuing.\n- `Menu` offers actions from a trigger, and stays anchored to it. On React Native the same\n  name is a bottom sheet, deliberately — a dropdown anchored to a trigger is not a native\n  idiom, and the shared contract is about vocabulary, not about pixels.\n- `Lightbox` is for viewing one thing large, with the page still behind it.\n\nIf two dialogs can be mounted at once, give them distinct title ids. A single hardcoded\n`id=\"modal-title\"` is a real regression this library has already had.\n\n## Images\n\n`Image` renders **only** `https://` sources. Anything else renders the placeholder and no\n`<img>` element at all, so no request is made.\n\n```tsx\n<Image src={photo} alt=\"Foto produk\" />\n<Image src={pattern} decorative />\n```\n\n`alt` or `decorative`, never both and never neither. The check is case-insensitive and\nanchored to the start of the string — a bare `http://` or a relative path gives you a\nplaceholder, not a broken image, which is worth knowing when a source silently shows nothing.\n\n## Sizing, and what to do when a size is missing\n\nReach for the axis. If the axis has no value for what you need, the change is a contract\nentry and a component that reads it — not a class override at the call site.\n\nAn override at the call site is invisible to every guard in this repo, and it is how a scale\nends up being patched by every caller. That has happened here: a button size every call site\noverrode was a broken size, and the fix was the scale, not the call sites.\n",
  SHELL_GUIDELINES: "# Shell\n\nThe chrome around a screen: the frame, the bar across the top and the navigation column beside\nthe content. Open this section when you are asking *what holds the page*, not what goes in it.\n\nEverything here is slots. `AppShell` reaches for React and this package's own class table and\nnothing else — no store, no router, no feature gate — because a library frame that imported a\nrouter would be unusable by the second app that wanted a frame. That is asserted by\n`layouts.stories.test.tsx`, not merely intended.\n\n## Use this, not that\n\n**Let `AppShell` own the scroll flag; do not wire your own listener.** A header that gains a\nshadow once the content has moved has to know about a scroll in a node it did not render, and\nscroll events do not bubble — so the frame listens in the capture phase. It also skips targets\nthat are not vertical scrollers (a horizontal card rail always reports offset zero) and targets\noutside the content region, because `Sidebar` gives its list its own scroller and scrolling the\nnavigation would otherwise answer \"not scrolled\" for a page that is still scrolled. Two\nsurfaces in this repo arrived at that independently, which is why it moved here.\n\n**Do not pass a viewport size to `Header` or `Sidebar`.** Neither takes one. `Header`'s\npublished `Size: Mobile | Desktop` pair became one component that reflows at the package\nbreakpoint, because the two sizes differ in three measurements and a bar told it is the small\none on a wide screen is a bug nothing can catch. `Sidebar`'s published `Size` became a\n`collapsed` boolean for the same family of reason: a two-value axis whose values mean yes and\nno is a boolean wearing an enum.\n\n## Members\n\n- **AppShell** — the frame: four slots, a full-height column that does not itself scroll, and\n  a pinned overflow chain so the caller's scroller is the thing that scrolls.\n- **Header** — the top bar, mirroring all seven published `type` values, with `showBack`,\n  `showTab`, `showTextLeft` and `scrolled`.\n- **Sidebar** — the navigation column and its row, `SidebarMenuItem`, shipped from one module\n  and contracted together.\n\n## Worth knowing\n\n`Sidebar` is the one contract entry covering two exports, and it says so rather than leaving it\nto read as a name/prop mismatch: `state`, `active`, `expanded` and `nested` belong to the row,\n`collapsed` and `open` to the shell. Its `state` values are spelled `menu | submenu` against\nFigma's `Satate`, corrected deliberately so nobody restores the typo on their next read of the\ndesign file. Sign-out is not a prop at all — the footer slot takes the caller's own control.\n\n`Header` mirrors an axis it disagrees with, on purpose. Those seven values conflate three\nindependent facts — signed in or not, which product surface the bar frames, and what kind of\npage it is — so \"signed in, on a detail page\" cannot be said. It is carried whole anyway,\nbecause a library that half-adopts a published axis diverges from the file it mirrors, and the\nconflation is recorded so design can split it and the library can follow. Exactly one value,\n`temanAiChat`, has a real consumer today.\n\nNone of these three declares a `family`, so each is covered by its own guard pair rather than\nby a family guard. That is not optional: a family guard reconciles only against entries that\nclaim its family, so an unfamilied component is invisible to all four.\n",
} as const;

export type DocPageKey = keyof typeof DOC_PAGES;
